\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Commuting links split into characters}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{U} be a connection for the \vocabk{Wilson kernel}{fdip-0153} whose link
operators #{U_j(x)} commute pairwise over all sites and directions. There
is an orthogonal decomposition of #{F} into #{d=\dim F} common invariant
complex lines. On line #{k} every link acts by a scalar #{\zeta_j^{(k)}(x)}
of modulus one. With these scalars as links, let #{U^{(k)}} be the connection
on #{\mathbb C} for the same grid, #{S}, #{\Gamma_S} and #{a}.
Then #{U} is a \vocabk{regular connection}{fdip-0153} exactly when every
#{U^{(k)}} is regular. The positive and negative eigenvalue counts of
#{\Gamma K_U} are the respective sums of those for #{\Gamma K_{U^{(k)}}}.
For regular #{U},
##{\operatorname{ind}(U)=\sum_{k=1}^d\operatorname{ind}(U^{(k)}).}}

\proof{
\p{Each link and its inverse preserve every eigenspace of every other
link, by commutation. Diagonalize one unitary using the finite spectral
argument in \ref{fdip-014A}. Restrict the next unitary to each eigenspace
and diagonalize again. The restrictions are unitary because both the
operator and its inverse preserve the space. After the last of the
finitely many links, choose an orthonormal basis in each joint eigenspace.
The spans of the basis vectors give the asserted orthogonal lines.
Multiplication and inversion of the links multiply and invert their scalars
on each line, giving a character of the group they generate.}

\p{Identify each line with #{\mathbb C} by its chosen unit vector.
The [unitary shifts](fdip-009O) and their adjoints restrict to the shifts
and adjoints for the corresponding scalar links. The spin generators
and grading preserve every summand. Thus #{\Gamma K_U} is the orthogonal
direct sum of the #{\Gamma K_{U^{(k)}}}. Such a sum is invertible exactly
when all summands are, and its positive and negative spaces are the
respective sums. For regular #{U}, \ref{fdip-0155} now gives the formula.}
}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.exists_orthonormalBasis_apply_eq_smul_of_isStarNormal}
gives the orthonormal eigenbasis used at each single-unitary step.}
